            <div class="faq-container">
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        How is CTAK different from Intacs or ring segments?
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Intacs are plastic rings in fixed sizes - like buying shoes that "sort of" fit. 
                        CTAK uses actual human corneal tissue custom-cut to YOUR exact corneal shape. 
                        It's biocompatible (your body accepts it as natural tissue), 
                        completely customizable, and provides better visual outcomes. 
                        Many patients who failed with Intacs see great results with CTAK.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        Is there really no rejection risk?
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Correct! The tissue is gamma-irradiated, which removes ALL donor cells and DNA. 
                        What's left is pure collagen structure - like scaffolding. 
                        Your immune system doesn't recognize it as foreign because there's nothing to reject. 
                        This is completely different from a transplant which uses living tissue.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        Can CTAK help if I have severe keratoconus?
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes! CTAK works for mild, moderate, AND severe KC. 
                        The tissue is custom-designed to address YOUR specific cone shape and severity. 
                        Even very steep corneas (50-80 diopters) can be treated. 
                        The only cases that might not qualify are those with dense central scarring 
                        that already need a transplant.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        How long do the results last?
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>The tissue becomes a permanent part of your cornea - your body integrates it completely. 
                        Clinical trials show stable results at 5+ years and counting. 
                        Unlike contacts that you struggle with daily or Intacs that can shift, 
                        CTAK reshapes your cornea permanently. Most patients never need another procedure.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        What's recovery actually like? Be honest.
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
                        <p>Day 1: Eyes feel scratchy, light sensitive. Netflix and chill. 
                        Day 2-3: Improving quickly, can use phone/computer. 
                        Day 4-7: Back to work/school, vision getting clearer daily. 
                        Week 2: Basically normal, might still use drops. 
                        Month 1: Vision stabilizing, seeing improvements. 
                        It's WAY easier than transplant recovery (6-12 months) or dealing with hard contacts forever.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <div class="faq-question">
                        Will I still need glasses or contacts after?
                        <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-answer">
